Yes. The shared pattern is sequential traversal with a discrete role-change event.

In leapfrog, motion advances by physically passing over the next person; the ordering changes as one actor moves through the sequence. In Duck Duck Goose, attention advances symbolically—duck, duck, duck—until one node is marked “goose,” which triggers a state transition: chase, race, seat replacement, new selector.

Abstractly:

iterate across nodes → maintain default state → select one node → trigger transition → update roles/position → repeat

Leapfrog expresses the pattern spatially. Duck Duck Goose expresses it socially and symbolically.

That same operator shows up in things like token passing, round-robin scheduling, hot-potato games, leader rotation, musical chairs, even certain search algorithms: move through a set until a threshold/selection event changes who acts next.

The interesting part is that once you see the operator, the surface game stops mattering much. You can start translating between physical, social, computational, and narrative versions of the same structure.